DDMORL_model_based_cosine.ipynb — Change Log
Session date: 2026-03-27
================================================================================

CELL 4  — Data loading
  ADDED:  `if not file.endswith('.tar'): continue` guard at the top of the
          inner file loop.
  REASON: `extract_dir` was only defined inside `if file.endswith('.tar'):` but
          `pd.read_csv()` calls were outside it, causing NameError on any
          non-tar file present in the directory.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CELL 5  — ACTIONS list + SYS.reward()
  CHANGED: ACTIONS list from 14 entries [89.0, 95.0, ...165.0] to 16 entries
           [78.0, 83.0, 89.0, ...165.0] — two lower PCAP levels added.
  CHANGED: reward_2 from raw `ns` (unnormalized progress, ~0–300) to
           `ns / max(max_progress, 1e-8)` — normalized to [0,1].
  REASON:  Raw ns caused Q[:,1] to be ~100× larger than Q[:,0], so all
           Q-vectors clustered near the same angle and the cosine scalarization
           could not distinguish between preferences.
  ADDED:   Fallback for ns==0: `reward = np.array([ACTION_MAX, 0.0])` — worst-
           case (max power, zero progress) instead of all-zeros.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CELL 8  — Dataset build + reward normalization
  CHANGED: `t1 = float('-inf')` moved inside the `for trace` loop.
  REASON:  Was outside the loop, so the state window for the first PCAP
           interval of each new trace included timestamps from the previous
           trace, corrupting state features.

  ADDED:   Per-app bounds block — computes MAX_PROGRESS, MIN_PROGRESS,
           MAX_MPOWER, MIN_MPOWER from actual assembled data (not hardcoded).
  REASON:  Hardcoded bounds (e.g. 78–165 W) don't match the real observed
           range per app and per dataset split.

  ADDED:   Full reward normalization to [-1, 1]:
             reward[0] = -(2*p_norm  - 1)   # low power  → +1
             reward[1] =  (2*g_norm  - 1)   # high progress → +1
  REASON:  Without normalization the two reward components had very different
           scales, making the preference dot-product meaningless.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CELL 12 — Zero-state filter  [NEW CELL ADDED]
  ADDED:   `is_zero_state()` filter on `training_dataset_loaded`.
  REMOVES: ~65/381 rows (17%) where all state features are zero, arising from
           the first PCAP interval before any sensor data has arrived.
  REASON:  Zero-state rows associate arbitrary PCAP actions with a null state,
           adding noise and potentially biasing the Q-network.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CELL 15 — get_tensors()
  ADDED:   Preference augmentation block (aug_prob=0.5): randomly replaces
           state-derived preference with Uniform(0,1) sample during training.
  ADDED:   Return signature extended: now returns (s_with_prefs, a,
           ns_with_prefs, r, prefs_array, s, ns, app).

  ** STATUS: Preference augmentation is PENDING REMOVAL **
     The user identified this as conceptually wrong for DDMORL: preference is
     deterministically tied to state progress, so random (state, pref) pairs
     are impossible at inference time and will never yield optimal transitions.
     The fix (remove aug_prob block, keep only state-derived preference) was
     proposed but the tool call was rejected and has not yet been applied.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CELL 17 — project_qvalues_cql_sampled()
  REPLACED: Old implementation that used only F.smooth_l1_loss with no CQL
            regularization.
  ADDED:    CQL loss term:
              q_norms       = pred.view(B, A, 2).norm(dim=-1)   # [B, A]
              logsumexp_q   = torch.logsumexp(q_norms, dim=-1)  # [B]
              cql_loss      = mean(logsumexp_q - dataset_q_norm)
              loss = td_loss + cql_alpha * cql_loss
  ADDED:    `optimizer.zero_grad()` — was missing in the original.
  REASON:   Without CQL the network had no penalty for overestimating Q-values
            of out-of-dataset actions; logsumexp penalizes high Q-norms across
            all actions relative to the observed action.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

