mild_RL_controller.py — Change Log
Session date: 2026-03-27
================================================================================

SIGTERM / timeout handling  [NEW]
  ADDED:   `import signal`
  ADDED:   Module-level global `_active_process = None` — tracks the live
           benchmark subprocess so the signal handler can reach it.
  ADDED:   `_sigterm_handler(signum, frame)` — registered for both SIGTERM
           (sent by the bash `timeout` command) and SIGINT (Ctrl-C).
           On receipt it:
             1. Terminates `_active_process` (SIGTERM → 5 s grace → SIGKILL).
             2. Raises SystemExit(1) to unwind the stack through the
                try/finally block in experiment_for().
  REASON:  Previously a timeout killed the Python process instantly, leaving
           CSV files open/uncompressed and the benchmark subprocess running
           until the bash script's `pkill` cleaned it up.

experiment_for() — subprocess tracking  [CHANGED]
  ADDED:   `global _active_process` declaration.
  ADDED:   `_active_process = process` immediately after Popen(), so the
           signal handler always has a reference to the live subprocess.

experiment_for() — try/finally around control loop  [CHANGED]
  WRAPPED: The `while True` control loop in a try/finally block.
  FINALLY: Terminates the benchmark subprocess if still running (SIGTERM →
           5 s grace → SIGKILL), then flushes all open CSV file handles
           (power, progress, energy, PCAP, papi) before the `with open()`
           context manager closes them.
  MOVED:   `compress_files()` call to after the `with open()` block so files
           are fully closed before the tar archive is written.
  REASON:  Guarantees compression runs whether the experiment completes
           normally, times out (SIGTERM), or raises any other exception.

Control loop — busy-wait fix  [CHANGED]
  RESTORED: `time.sleep(0.1)` (was commented out).
  REASON:   Without the sleep the loop spun at 100% CPU, competing with the
            benchmark process and inflating its runtime.

